capability dotenv/parse  a `.env` text read into its names and values
  heroes  config.parse_dotenv
  go      godotenv.Parse, Unmarshal, UnmarshalBytes
  rust    dotenvy::from_read_iter, Iter, Error, Error::LineParse
  python  dotenv.dotenv_values(stream=), dotenv.main.DotEnv, DotEnv.dict, DotEnv.get, DotEnv.parse, parse_stream, Binding
  note    python-dotenv's reading, where the three disagree: a name holding `.` or `-` (godotenv and dotenvy refuse `-`), a quoted name, `\t` and `\a \b \f \v` read between double quotes (godotenv writes `t`, dotenvy refuses), an unknown escape kept with its backslash, `\'` and `\\` read between single quotes (godotenv keeps them), `A=#x` a value and `B= #y` empty, a byte order mark skipped. Refused where python-dotenv skips the line with a warning or reads `None`: a name with no `=`, `export` alone, `KEY: value` (godotenv reads it), text after a closing quote. A name written twice keeps its last value at its first place, as godotenv and python-dotenv answer

capability dotenv/read  a `.env` file read by its path
  heroes  config.read_dotenv
  go      godotenv.Read
  rust    dotenvy::from_path_iter, from_filename_iter, dotenv_iter, Error::Io
  python  dotenv.dotenv_values(dotenv_path=), get_key
  note    -

capability dotenv/expand  `${NAME}` in a value replaced by an earlier value or a variable
  heroes  config.parse_dotenv_with, config.read_dotenv_with, config.DotenvOptions
  go      godotenv's expansion, always on
  rust    dotenvy's substitution, always on
  python  dotenv_values(interpolate=), resolve_variables, parse_variables, dotenv.variables
  note    only when asked, as python-dotenv's `interpolate`; `${NAME}` and `${NAME:-default}` alone, a bare `$NAME` kept as text as python-dotenv keeps it (godotenv and dotenvy replace it); never between single quotes (python-dotenv replaces there, godotenv and dotenvy do not); `\$` writes `$` (godotenv, dotenvy). A name from a line above wins over the environment, as godotenv and python-dotenv's `dotenv_values` answer (dotenvy takes the environment's). `:-` gives the default where the value is unset or empty, as POSIX's shell; python-dotenv gives it only where unset. A `${` with no `}` or no name is refused, which python-dotenv keeps as text

capability dotenv/load  a `.env` file's values written into the process's environment
  heroes  refused: the environment is read and never written (README § A5): in C a write beside another thread's read is undefined. A `.env` file's values become a layer through `from_env`, or a child's environment through `process`
  go      godotenv.Load, Overload
